    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "Find ${y}^{\u2032}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2)}{4x}.$",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2)$ and the denominator $v(x)=4x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=7{sec}^{2}\u2061(7x-2),\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{sec}^{2}\u2061(7x-2))(4x)-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(7x{sec}^{2}\u2061(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2))}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{\u29f8}4(7x{sec}^{2}\u2061(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2))}{\\text{\u29f8}4(4{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}\u2061(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
