    "source_id": "q-2400",
    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "fields": [],
