  {
    "source_id": "q-2403",
    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "Find ${y}^{\u2032}$ for $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}}{2x}.$",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}},\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}})(2x)-(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x})(2)}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}}-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
