    "source_id": "q-313365",
    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(x+3)}^{2}$ |",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+3.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)\\cdot (x+3)-(x+1)^{2}\\cdot 1}{(x+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2(x+3)-(x+1)]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2x+6-x-1]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+5)}{{(x+3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "fields": [
