    "source_id": "q-343576",
    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{\u2032}(1).$\n\n${y}^{\u2032}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{\u2032}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
