    "source_id": "q-344143",
    "level": "moderate",
    "original_problem": "Find ${y}^{\u2032}$ for $y=\\frac{{e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{x-2}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\u2032}$ | $=$ | $(${{field-1}}$){e}^{3{x}^{2}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{2}$ |",
    "original_worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3{x}^{2}}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x-2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}(x)=6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}},\\,{v}^{\u2032}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}})(x-2)-({e}^{3{x}^{2}})(1)}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3{x}^{2}}(6x(x-2)-1)}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6{x}^{2}-12x-1){e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{(x-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
